11	// Regression safeguards for the sliding-window event count at the signed
12	// 64-bit limit. Each (key, window) keeps an independent count of the events it
13	// actually received: a normally accepted event -- positive, negative or zero
14	// value alike -- counts once in every window that contains its event time,
15	// independently of that window's sum. An addition that lands the count exactly
16	// on math.MaxInt64 is legal and must close with that exact integer rendered as
17	// a JSON number (no rounding, no sign flip); only the NEXT accepted event,
18	// which would need math.MaxInt64+1 counts, is fatal at its own physical input
19	// line as an *InputError that says "event count overflow" and names the key
20	// and the offending window's left-closed/right-open interval. Overlapping
21	// windows fail independently, and the late-event check runs first, so a
22	// strictly late event is merely skipped (with the existing notice) even when a
23	// still-open window it lands in is already at the count limit. Scope is the
24	// legacy single-watermark RunAggregateSliding entry point.
25	//
26	// The windows under test are seeded straight at the boundary through package
27	// internals: feeding 9223372036854775806 events cannot be done at a finite
28	// offline scale, whereas one seeded state plus a handful of real records read
29	// through the production line collector exercises the exact update loop,
30	// physical line counting (blank lines count), watermark closure and output
31	// formatting.
